ontact, or accidental or iatrogenic exposure (e.g., needle","id":54,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false},{"t":" "},{"t":"puncture injury to medical personnel). HIV infects several types of cells, most particularly “T-","id":55,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false},{"t":" "},{"t":"helper” (CD4) lymphocytes and monocytes. Over time, the infection can cause severe decreases","id":56,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false},{"t":" "},{"t":"in the CD4 count, resulting in severe immunocompromise, often leading to opportunistic","id":57,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false},{"t":" "},{"t":"infections and neoplasms. Infected monocytes can enter the central nervous system, leading to","id":58,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false},{"t":" "},{"t":"infection of macrophages and microglia. A small percentage of astrocytes may harbor productive","id":59,"b":false,"i":false,"s":false}],"level":4,"bbox":[72.0,604.65,539.97,717.45],"firstLineX":72.0,"indent":0,"small":false}],"figures":[],"ocr":null,"nativeTextSha256":"487277c9785a4e95ef35aa9ac41aad85ce83cddebb99ac9715a758adc49c5b2e","nativeCharacters":2502,"sparse":false,"tableCount":0}